<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">37–<a href="/years/68">68 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Nero Claudius Caesar Augustus Germanicus · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julio-claudian-dynasty">Julio-Claudian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nero was Roman emperor from 54 to 68 CE, the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julio-claudian-dynasty">Julio-Claudian dynasty</a>, adopted heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>. His reign saw the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the settlement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> that placed Tiridates on the Armenian throne, the Great Fire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 64 and the persecution of Christians. Revolts by provincial governors led to his fall, and he took his own life in 68.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nero.jpg" alt="Nero" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nero_Glyptothek_Munich_321.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nero was born Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus on 15 December 37 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antium">Antium</a>, the only child of Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus and Agrippina the Younger. His uncle was the reigning emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>, and through his mother he was a descendant of Augustus. His father died when he was three. By the time he was eleven, his mother had married the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, who adopted him as his heir. When Claudius died in 54, Nero came to the throne at the age of sixteen with the backing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praetorian-guard">Praetorian Guard</a> and the Senate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In his early years Nero was guided by his mother, his tutor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-the-younger">Seneca the Younger</a> and the praetorian prefect Sextus Afranius Burrus. His first speech to the Senate, prepared by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, promised to end secret trials and to respect the privileges of senators. Agrippina meant to rule through her son, and the struggle between them ended with her murder in 59. Roman sources also implicate him in the deaths of his stepbrother Britannicus and his wife Claudia Octavia. Modern scholars think the reign went well before Agrippina's death, and a successful expedition explored the sources of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>.</p><p>The general Corbulo fought the Roman–Parthian War of 58 to 63 and made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>. Suetonius Paulinus defeated a major revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> led by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a>, who had burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a>. The Great Fire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> began on the night of 18 to 19 July 64 and destroyed three of the city's fourteen districts and severely damaged seven more. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> records a belief that Nero started it to clear land for his Golden House, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> says he seized Christians as scapegoats and had them burned alive. In 65 a conspiracy led by Gaius Calpurnius Piso failed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> and the poet Lucan were among those who died. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a> began in 66.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In March 68 Gaius Julius Vindex, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-lugdunensis">Gallia Lugdunensis</a>, rebelled against Nero's tax policies and called on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Servius Sulpicius Galba</a> to declare himself emperor. Verginius Rufus defeated Vindex at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Vesontio</a> in May, but support for Galba grew, the Praetorian prefect Nymphidius Sabinus deserted Nero, and he was declared a public enemy. Nero fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and killed himself in 68, which began the civil wars of the Year of the Four Emperors.</p><p>Most surviving sources, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, are hostile, but none was written during his reign, and some modern historians question their reliability, noting his popularity among ordinary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. In the eastern provinces a legend arose that he had not died, and at least three leaders of short-lived rebellions presented themselves as Nero reborn.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nero” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vast estates were common; Pliny the Elder reports that six landowners held half the province until <b>Nero</b> had them put to death and their land passed to the emperor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 66 CE <b>Nero</b> crowned Tiridates I, a Parthian Arsacid, under the Treaty of Rhandeia of 63; contemporary Roman writers thought Nero had in fact yielded Armenia to the Parthians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Pompey installed Aristarchus as dynast (63–47 BCE); under Polemon I, Pythodorida of Pontus and Polemon II Colchis belonged to Pontus until <b>Nero</b> induced Polemon to abdicate and the land was absorbed into the province of Galatia in 63 and Cappadocia in 81.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its best-known governor was Marcus Salvius Otho, sent to Lusitania in 58 CE by <b>Nero</b>, who had taken Otho's wife Poppaea Sabina; Otho governed for a decade with a reputation for moderation, supported Galba's revolt in 68 and briefly became emperor in 69.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Paul was executed in Rome, by tradition under <b>Nero</b>, as was Peter.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Jews were at times exempt from official sacrifice, but Christians were persecuted: by <b>Nero</b> after the fire of 64 CE, under Decius’s edict of sacrifice, by Valerian, and by Diocletian’s edicts from 303, until Galerius revoked the policy in 311.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antium">Antium</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.45,12.63&amp;year=57" aria-label="Antium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4858 686 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nero"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5308 956h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5308 956h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.45,12.63&amp;year=57">What was here in 57 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
